 of us  yeah in the 21st century I'm thinking in  particular and I think also this is  probably brought into sharp focus  because if you step back yet again and  you ask yourself where's the 21st  century going and just to take one  example if China continues to go down  the development path that it's going  down now let's call it the Western  development path  the best numbers I've seen show that  China within 20 years well or 20 let's  say around 2030 something like that the  per capita income on a purchasing power  basis will be the equivalent of the per  capita income in the United States today  so if that were the case and the Chinese  let's say broadly speaking behaved as  Americans did or Western developed  countries did then for every four people  you'd have three automobiles which means  you have one point 1 billion automobiles  today in the world we have 800 million  in total China would be consuming 100  million barrels of oil a day today in  the entire world we produced maybe 19  million barrels a day so this one  country China you know most every  feature and dimension would be consuming  more than the whole world does today now  if you follow that logic and you then  throw in and what India might be doing  or Brazil or Russia or Indonesia let  alone where the United States develops  in Western Europe you quickly come to a  view that this doesn't work and  therefore you need some kind of new  model which the Chinese leadership is  conscious of and so when you go down  that path and you say what does that new  model mean what does it look like  you can quickly come to the question  what does it mean to be a fulfilled or a  successful person does it mean is it a  consumer society you know I was reading  a bit of a Marin history the other day  in the 17th 18th century and noted that  around that time the leaders in this  country told people or encouraged people  to think that consuming was in and of  itself as social good and to this day  American policymakers will tell the  Chinese you need to get your people to  consume more if you would do that would  be good for the world economy which  undoubtedly is correct but it does beg  the question it where's it written that  consumption is dipso facto a good thing  and where's the balance here between  what you consume and how you consume it  and how its manufactured  and so forth so it seems pretty clear  that a new model is going to have to  evolve now that new model in my own mind  isn't just for China it's going to have  to be for the rest of us and that's  going to be quite dramatic change so in  that context coming back to the question  of spirituality I think you do come full  circle because you start to think harder  and sort of redefine or put different  emphasis on what it means to what a  fulfilling life looks like it's not  simply it's really not just a  materialistic and at the moment that's  where the Chinese society seems to be  heading and it's not just a  materialistic and then what is it and  what is it that gives people deeper  satisfaction so so in the u.s. the  sources of those models would be  religious leaders they they might be  people who we'd put broadly under the  rubric of social entrepreneurs  developing developing a model create  creating a constituency for that model  and then trying to get that model into  the public sphere for dialogue debate  and and hopefully at least for that  entrepreneur it would it would it would  it would spread um how does that happen  in China I mean when I go back to what  you were saying about the harmonious  society it sounds like this is a  dialogue that takes place within the  leadership that sort of gets tried out  and they wait for reaction and see how  it plays out modify based on that  reaction is that is that how it works  yeah I think the Chinese leadership for  some time have had this notion of sort  of what I'll call big ideas that every  so often get put out and initially when  they're put out they are relatively  ill-defined and there's kind of an  unspoken conversation that goes on and  sometimes that conversation is very  vivid and very very animated and very  energetic and you can see it and hear it  and sometimes it's more subtle than that  but  the conversation takes shape over time  and and then the leadership sort of  refines the concept or the concept  becomes an effect in effect collectively  refined so for example just a few years  ago the Chinese leaders have referred to  China's peaceful rise which was another  kind of term of art and is that that  particular term was debated among  intellectuals in particular both  intellectuals on the left and the right  disliked it because on the one hand the  ones on the Left said rise peaceful rise  why are we why are we using a term like  that which might circumscribed  our ability to behave in foreign policy  terms they might in effect be a form of  unilateral disarmament and you had  intellectuals on the right saying this  word rise this word rises to threatening  we should use this word rise so  eventually peaceful rise morphed into  peaceful development and now that term  of art still exists so with harmonious  society I think when it was first put  out there many people were sort of  somewhere between particularly  Westerners I would say we're sort of  dismissive of it this is kind of a form  of propaganda doesn't mean anything etc  I think actually is this lived on it's  taken on greater force because as I said  a minute ago I do think there is  increasing clarity of understanding that  simply going down the western road won't  be enough and with there's attention  because the Chinese have lifted  depending on the numbers somewhere  between 250 to 400 million people out of  poverty in essentially 30 years which is  quite an extraordinary achievement and  virtually everyone in the country will  know someone in his son or daughter or  aunt or uncle or somebody has done very  well and so therefore the hundreds of  millions who have not yet been pulled  out of poverty are sort of hoping and  assuming they will be pulled out of  poverty and so the Chinese leadership  have got to deliver on that but on the  other hand they've got to deliver on in  the way that's that's good for the  overall it was a very very tough  tasks which also circles back to why  stability is so important because they  say to themselves we absolutely it's  absolutely essential that we have  stability in order to give us the  luxuries or or the room to maneuver so  that we can make some of these  trade-offs that we're going to have to  make in a way that that works so if you  had been teaching your class in the US  and you had been asking your students to  sort of question you know what's their  purpose why are they there what what do  they want to achieve you would be I  assume expecting hoping wanting that the  students would be sort of looking out to  the you know many sources to help guide  them through that answer in China how  important is it that the that the  leadership worked through the model for  your students to be able to come up with  an answer that they feel comfortable  about well let me give you two of two  thoughts on that both my students and  also the ordinary people let me start  with a second first there is a woman  professor she's actually professor of  media at a at a Chinese University who  did her master's degree on Confucius and  in the past two years she's gone on  television sort of a kind of a Chinese  equivalent of a a form of Oprah Winfrey  let's say she's on television talking to  ordinary people about Confucius and  their relevance of Confucius to ordinary  people in ordinary circumstances it sort  of Confucius for the masses and she's  also put out a book and the book has  sold 10 million copies that have been  acquired been bought and ten million  that have been pirated the best-selling  book in China now that tells you  something it tells you I think that that  ordinary people are looking for someone  to come along and give them a framework  of some kind that helps put their life  into some larger context  now for my students who are by  definition part of the elite they I  think it's much more complex because  they have access to and they are hungry  for information from all over the world  almost all of my students are are good  English speakers most if not all have  traveled quite extensively or have  traveled quite extensively they are in  veteran users of the Internet so they're  looking all over the place and and and I  think that kind of exuberant or  energetic experimentation is going to  continue for a while and therefore with  the Chinese leadership so is only some  things that you were talking about which  was pride as opposed to the focus on  clear and the clear one solution in the  values spirituality and having a model  of self here gives me very very hungry  there's much more concern about  organized religion and and again for the  exact same reason about its concerned  because of the potential impact that it  can have on on stability I mean if I if  I put those two together it sounds in  some sense like in China the the the the  evolution of of people's spiritual  selves will be based more on this  country level dialogue than on any  actual action I mean if I can be a  little bit self-deprecating mean sounds  kind of like an academics paradise right  I mean it sort of like we'll just sort  of keep talking and when we get it we  get it as opposed to you need to build  churches you need to build organizations  you've got to think about you know what  what do you have to have in place in  terms of interests is that is that yeah  well first of all I would say I'm sure  it I just don't know enough about this  but I'm sure there are individuals who  have in their mind what might be called  a conventional model and they're  involved with a specific church and that  church is building itself out across the  country and so on I'm sure there is some  of that going on  but I would say in all my time in China  very rarely do you run across someone  unless you seek that person out who in  the ordinary course of talking with you  or even in a more detailed in-depth  conversation that I might have with the  student as I'm mentoring him or her very  rarely will they come straight out and  say you know I'm a committed Christian  and I'd like to talk to you about that  now there are some like that I have one  student for example who whose parents  were in the government he was in the  government and he literally says that he  was walking down Park Avenue one day in  the early 90s and he had a revelation  and right there on the spot he decided  to leave his government job he went back  to China he left his job and he started  a business which manufactures Christmas  trees electronic Christmas trees which  he sells through Walmart to make enough  money to come back and go to Yale Law  School to study constitutional law to  return to China to have an impact on the  work on the development of the rule of  law but that's one student out of let's  say 500 I've taught in the last six  years now there are a fair number of  students or friends who are buddhists of  one buddhist of one sort or another when  I said one sort or another I mean  varying degrees of spirituality as  distinct from kind of an ethical  framework which is part of their makeup  but even there this is not a topic that  they would typically volunteer this is a  topic that you would you might get into  because you sought it out so and I think  part of this is just cultural that  cultural in the sense that the Chinese  culture isn't the kind of culture the  word that would be volunteers it might  be in certain other cultures and part of  a cultural meaning the society is  developing and so that what's  permissible and what sort of convention  is is evolving and so I think it's  correct my point might find a kind of a  prognosticator I would guess that  there'll be another quite a few years  well this  station we've been describing is taking  place well at the same time certain  things are happening within organized  religion which is probably in some sense  reinforcing that conversation or feeding  into the conversation and and a lot will  depend on how those organized religions  conduct themselves do they I mean you  could if you wanted to be wanted to be  expansive you might even say that the  that the strict separation of church and  state might evolve for a very different  reason in China as it did here and  what's what's what's permissible in the  public square and what form and and how  organized is it and I think just to make  one other footnote you've also seen the  Chinese leadership I think try harder in  dealing with organized religion such as  the Catholic Church in trying to come to  some some kind of accommodation I'm sure  you know that there's been a kind of  free zone between the Chinese and the  Chi Catholic Church for a long time  which has started a thought and I think  the Chinese in general have taken the  view that it's in China's interest to  engage in dialogue and reach some kind  of harmonious state whether that's with  other state actors or other other  institutional actors and there are only  a couple of issues that are kind of sort  of non-negotiable or no-go areas where  therefore the review is crystal clear  and it's black and white and can't be  transgressed but other than those few  issues there general operating  philosophy has been to reach out to  people institutions and states in order  to create the conditions under which  they can govern the society which is  itself such a complex task that they  can't afford the distraction effect of  dis harmonious relations with anybody so  so so one last question you and I have  talked before about China one of the  things that you have commented on is the  the very long time horizon that the  Chinese leadership  for those who would be observing China  would be interested in the way in which  the dynamics around spirituality and  religion would unfold what's the time  line they should be thinking about to  get some kind of indication as to to  where things are going would you expect  in the next two years that things could  change in a significant way five years  ten years no let's see the way I think  about this I mean one one marker I spent  a long time researching and ultimately  writing a piece on political evolution  in China and in effect what that piece  said was that in my judgment some time  in the fifth generation of leadership  the Chinese since the Revolution first  generation was now second generation was  done she'll paying third was Chun  semaine fourth as our current leadership  of jeantel so the fifth generation idea  the next leaders would who come into  power in 2012 and then they're in power  for ten years so from 2012 to 2022 my  piece on political evolution essentially  said my guess is sometime in that time  period we'll start to get a clearer  sense of where political evolution is  really going and wherever it's going  it'll be distinctly Chinese and put a  little trend towards trend towards a  Western democratic model I think in  parallel that will be issues like this  question of spirituality it'll be there  for two reasons one because I think the  leadership will see that spirituality  can be a congealing force and  necessarily a a force which works  against the stability of the state it  can be a congealing force and also a  force in some ways that can moderate  other more potentially pernicious issues  like nationalism like unbridled  nationalism which the Chinese think of  to always have to be on guard for so I  think they will realize that and I think  also they'll just generally become  increasingly pluralistic and recognized  that's part of the texture of a of us of  a 21st century society that's  thing and so I think in there will be  this as when to watch for this but there  will not be and my judgment anyway there  won't be a kind of a time where it goes  from we turn on a light switch it goes  from from dark to light it'll just sort  of be slowly coming out of the out of  the ether all right well done thank you  so much for sharing your thoughts  sharing your insights and for  participating in the seminar once again  I'm Joel padule-- and have had the  pleasure to have conversation with John  Thornton thank you thank you
 

